course: dictionary
lesson: 9781
title: Surprisingly: when things are not what we expect
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Surprisingly» is an adverb. It tells you how something happens: in a way that is not expected. «Surprisingly, it was free» means you thought it would cost money, but it did not.
explain: Look for the clues around it. In «proving it 100% is surprisingly hard», you might expect proof to be easy, so surprisingly warns you that the difficulty is a surprise.
explain: Use the swap check. Put «not what I expected» in place of the word and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have the right meaning.
fact: Surprisingly means in a way that is not expected.
fact: «Surprisingly» often signals a twist in a story or report: «surprisingly, the smallest dog won».
fact: «Not surprisingly» means the opposite: what happened was expected.
fact: Other common phrases are «surprisingly well», «surprisingly easy» and «surprisingly enough».
fact: In science, «surprisingly» can mark a result that went against the prediction.
exercise: «Doing nothing on purpose is surprisingly hard.» What does surprisingly mean here? | Quietly and without fuss | On purpose and with a plan | Very slowly and carefully | *In a way that is not expected
exercise: «Surprisingly, the smallest dog won.» What does surprisingly tell the reader? | It happened every year | It was the biggest race ever | It was what everyone wanted | *It was not what people expected
exercise: Which phrase shows that a result was expected? | surprisingly well | *not surprisingly | surprisingly enough | surprisingly easy
test: In «the floor is often surprisingly easy to walk across», what does surprisingly tell you? | The floor is made of glass | It is always raining there | Walking there is slower than usual | *Walking there is easier than you would expect
test: «Many rainforest soils are surprisingly poor.» Which meaning is best? | They are wet and muddy like rain | They are richer than any other soil | They are very hard rock | *They have fewer nutrients than you might expect
exam: Jayden says «surprisingly» is just a filler word that does nothing. Why might an author use it? | To show a word has been spelled wrong | *To show that something is not what people would expect | To make the sentence longer for no reason | To mean very fast
